WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.725
What happens if a task estimate changes?

00:00:03.845 --> 00:00:07.045
Open Plan and choose What-if scenario.

00:00:07.165 --> 00:00:11.190
The app creates a draft based on a snapshot of the current
plan.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:16.535
Editing that draft leaves the document unchanged until you
review and apply it.

00:00:16.655 --> 00:00:20.405
Use this space to test a specific planning assumption.

00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.108
Here we explore a smaller Backend estimate.

00:00:27.228 --> 00:00:31.303
Select Backend and set Scenario duration to eight days.

00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:35.923
Choose Update scenario to recalculate the draft and its
impact.

00:00:36.043 --> 00:00:39.693
Compare the downstream schedule and release milestone.

00:00:39.813 --> 00:00:45.863
An earlier date depends on the assumption being
achievable, so explain what changed in the work.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:52.267
Give the alternative a useful name.

00:00:52.387 --> 00:00:55.862
First update the task estimate in the scenario.

00:00:55.982 --> 00:01:00.807
Add a name such as Reduced backend scope and write down
the assumption.

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:03.452
Save it as a new scenario.

00:01:03.572 --> 00:01:07.847
A saved alternative retains the basis for the comparison.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:12.117
It is a proposal to review, not an automatic improvement.

00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:19.333
When the proposal is ready, choose Review and apply.

00:01:19.453 --> 00:01:23.528
Inspect the source patch and the reported scheduling
impact.

00:01:23.648 --> 00:01:27.023
Check that the changed tasks match your intention.

00:01:27.143 --> 00:01:30.643
Resolve any warnings before committing the draft.

00:01:30.763 --> 00:01:34.713
Apply scenario writes the reviewed changes into the plan.

00:01:34.833 --> 00:01:40.508
The source now records the eight day estimate, so the
accepted decision stays visible.
